<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.10.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01020</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.10.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>54ebdb63-db87-4296-9aa2-8301376699b9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>Use the following scenario and graph to answer question 1. </para>
<blockquote>
    <para>Functions \(R\) and \(D\) give the height, in feet, of a toy rocket and a drone, \(t\) seconds after they are released.</para>
</blockquote>
<para>Here are the graphs of \(R\) (for the rocket) and \(D\) (for the drone).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Two functions graphed on a cooridnate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6953fee5f8d02223583c335e6d1949b8e372de5f"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
   <item>Decide whether the following statements are true or false.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(R(3)&gt;D(3)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> True</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(R(0)=D(0)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> False</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(R(4.5)=D(4.5)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>True</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(R(0)=D(0)+25\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>True</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(D(7) &gt; R(5)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> False</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(A(t)\) is the average high temperature in Aspen, Colorado, \(t\) months after the start of the year. \(M(t)\) is the average high temperature in Minneapolis, Minnesota, \(t\) months after the start of the year. Temperature is measured in degrees Fahrenheit.
     <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7b3766d3ead874b4e2db8f1013d3f0b5420120a9"/></media></figure><para>Which function had the higher average rate of change between the beginning of January and middle of March? </para>
    </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Minneapolis</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here are two graphs representing functions \(f\) and \(g\).</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/30220e31ee1b23d05fb738dae100f8116e999253"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Select the three </emphasis>statements that are true about functions \(f\) and \(g\).</para>
<list type="a">
  <item>\(f(0)&gt;g(0)\)</item>
  <item>There are values of \(x\) where \(f(x)=g(x)\).</item>
  <item>\(f(-1)&gt;g(-1)\)</item>
  <item>\(f(-3)&gt;g(4)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>There are values of \(x\) where \(f(x)=g(x)\), \(f(-1)&gt;g(-1)\), \(f(-3)&gt;g(4)\)</para>
<para>For questions 4 – 6, use the three graphs below:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>The three graphs represent the progress of three runners in a 400-meter race.
    The solid line represents Runner A. The dotted line represents Runner B. The dashed line represents Runner C.</para>
</blockquote>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c921ccce5023a038cae710698ab652dcf8a30c16"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which runner ran at a constant rate throughout the race? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Runner C </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which runner stopped running for a while? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Runner A</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> Which runner won the race? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Runner C</para>
<para>Use the graphs below for questions 7 – 8.</para>
<para>Show A                                             Show B                                   Show C</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4ac31af5cda20f893e4a37940e125dea4f2d0f23"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which show had the largest audience for episode 4? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Show B</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which show had the largest audience for their first episode? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Show B</para></section>
